#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186364275 :question/problem "Evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }12x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let's use integration by parts. We set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=12x & ⇒ & {u}^{′}=12 \\\\ {v}^{′}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x) & ⇒ & v=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the integration by parts formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int 12x\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =12x\\cdot (-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x))-\\int (-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x))\\cdot 12\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }12x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =-12x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x){|}_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }+12{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-12x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x){|}_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }+12\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x){|}_{-\\pi }^{\\pi } \\\\  & =-12[\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi )+\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(-\\pi )] \\\\  & +12[\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi )-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-\\pi )] \\\\  & =-12[-\\pi -\\pi ]+12[0-0] \\\\  & =-12[-2\\pi ] \\\\  & =24\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186364309 :question/problem "Consider the plane curve $x+1={x}^{2}y.$ Find a tangent vector to that curve at the point $(1,2).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+1 & ={x}^{2}y \\\\ {x}^{2}y-x-1 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y)={x}^{2}y-x-1$, our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=0.$ \n\nA tangent vector to the level curve that passes through the point $(1,2)$ can be computed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{t}(1,2)={f}_{y}(1,2)\\mathbf{i}-{f}_{x}(1,2)\\mathbf{j}\n$$\n\nSo, we calculate $\\mathbf{t}(x,y)$ and evaluate it at $(1,2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}y-x-1)i-\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}y-x-1)j \\\\  & ={x}^{2}\\,i-(2xy-1)j \\\\ t(1,2) & =(1)^{2}\\,i-(2(1)(2)-1)j \\\\  & =i-3\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our tangent vector is $\\mathbf{i}-3\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186364316 :question/problem "At which points on the curve $2xy-3{x}^{2}=-y$ is the tangent vector to the curve perpendicular to the vector $\\mathbf{j}?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2xy-3{x}^{2}=-y \\\\ 2xy-3{x}^{2}+y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y)=2xy-3{x}^{2}+y$, our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=0.$ \n\nA tangent vector to the level curve can be computed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{t}(x,y)={f}_{y}(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-{f}_{x}(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}\n$$\n\nSo, we calculate $\\mathbf{t}(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{t}(x,y) & ={f}_{y}(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-{f}_{x}(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2xy-3{x}^{2}+y)\\mathbf{i}-\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2xy-3{x}^{2}+y)\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =(2x+1)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-2(y-3x)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince our tangent vector needs to be perpendicular to $\\mathbf{j}$, we must have $\\mathbf{t}\\cdot \\mathbf{j}=0.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{t}(x,y)\\cdot \\mathbf{j} & =0 \\\\ ((2x+1)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-2(y-3x)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j})\\cdot \\mathbf{j} & =0 \\\\ -2(y-3x) & =0 \\\\ y & =3x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $y=3x$ into the equation of our curve gives:  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2xy-3{x}^{2}+y & =0 \\\\ 2x(3x)-3{x}^{2}+3x & =0 \\\\ 6{x}^{2}-3{x}^{2}+3x & =0 \\\\ 3{x}^{2}+3x & =0 \\\\ 3x(x+1) & =0 \\\\ x & =0\\,\\text{or}\\,x=-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally:\n\n- If $x=0$, we get $y=0$ and the corresponding point is $(0,0).$\n\n- If $x=-1$, we get $y=-3$ and the corresponding point is $(-1,-3).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186364948 :question/problem "Find the value of $$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{vmatrix}7 & 1 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 1 \\\\ -3 & 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Using the formula, we take the alternating sum of the entries in the top row of the matrix, multiplied by their respective minors, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}7 & 1 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 1 \\\\ -3 & 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix} & =7\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix}-1\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{vmatrix}-3\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =7(6-1)-1(0+3)-3(0+9) \\\\  & =7\\cdot 5-1\\cdot 3-3\\cdot 9 \\\\  & =35-3-27 \\\\  & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365398 :question/problem "$$\n\\mathbf{f}(x,z)=⟨x,\\hspace{0.222em}6{x}^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{___}⟩\n$$\n\nThe vector-valued function $\\mathbf{f}(x,z)$ above parametrizes the parabolic cylinder defined in Cartesian coordinates as $y=6{x}^{2}.$ What is the third component of $\\mathbf{f}?$\n\nUse the free parameter directly as its corresponding Cartesian coordinate." :question/worked-solution "For any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{f}(x,z) & =⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨x,\\hspace{0.222em}6{x}^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}{\\color{blue}z}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )$ and $z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nTherefore, the third component of $\\mathbf{f}$ is ${\\color{blue}z}.$\n\nHere the free parameter is $z$. The stated direct-coordinate convention therefore requires the third component to be $z$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365405 :question/problem "$$\n\\mathbf{f}(y,z)=⟨\\text{___},\\hspace{0.222em}y,\\hspace{0.222em}z⟩\n$$\n\nThe vector-valued function $\\mathbf{f}(y,z)$ above parametrizes the parabolic cylinder defined in Cartesian coordinates as $x=3{y}^{2}.$ What is the first component of $\\mathbf{f}?$" :question/worked-solution "For any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{f}(y,z) & =⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{\\color{blue}3{y}^{2}},\\hspace{0.222em}y,\\hspace{0.222em}z⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $y\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )$ and $z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nTherefore, the first component of $\\mathbf{f}$ is ${\\color{blue}3{y}^{2}}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365412 :question/problem "$$\n\\mathbf{f}(x,t)=⟨x,\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{_____},\\hspace{0.167em}7\\operatorname{tan}t⟩,\\hspace{2em}t\\in (-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2})\n$$\n\nThe vector-valued function $\\mathbf{f}(x,t)$ above parametrizes one sheet of the hyperbolic cylinder defined in Cartesian coordinates as ${y}^{2}-{z}^{2}=49$ for $y\\ge 7.$ What is the second component of $\\mathbf{f}?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the hyperbola ${y}^{2}-{z}^{2}=49$ for $y\\ge 7$ in $yz$-plane has the parametrization\n\n$$\n⟨y,z⟩=⟨7\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\\,7\\operatorname{tan}⁡t⟩,\\,t\\in (-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2})\n$$\n\nsince \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}-{z}^{2} & =(7\\operatorname{sec}⁡t)^{2}-(7\\operatorname{tan}⁡t)^{2} \\\\  & =49{sec}^{2}⁡t-49{tan}^{2}⁡t \\\\  & =49({sec}^{2}⁡t-{tan}^{2}⁡t) \\\\  & =49.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, for any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{f}(x,t) & =⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨x,\\hspace{0.167em}{\\color{blue}7\\operatorname{sec}t},\\hspace{0.167em}7\\operatorname{tan}t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2})$ and $x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nTherefore, the second component of $\\mathbf{f}$ is ${\\color{blue}7\\operatorname{sec}t}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365419 :question/problem "$$\n\\mathbf{f}(t,y)=⟨\\text{_____},\\hspace{0.167em}y,\\hspace{0.167em}4\\operatorname{tan}t⟩,\\hspace{2em}t\\in (-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2})\n$$\n\nThe vector-valued function $\\mathbf{f}(t,y)$ above parametrizes one sheet of the hyperbolic cylinder defined in Cartesian coordinates as ${x}^{2}-\\frac{{z}^{2}}{16}=1$ for $x\\ge 1.$ What is the first component of $\\mathbf{f}?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the hyperbola ${x}^{2}-\\frac{{z}^{2}}{16}=1$ for $x\\ge 1$ in $xz$-plane has the parametrization\n\n$$\n⟨x,z⟩=⟨\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\\,4\\operatorname{tan}⁡t⟩,\\,t\\in (-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2})\n$$\n\nsince \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-\\frac{{z}^{2}}{16} & =(\\operatorname{sec}⁡t)^{2}-\\frac{(4\\operatorname{tan}⁡t)^{2}}{16} \\\\  & ={sec}^{2}⁡t-\\frac{16{tan}^{2}⁡t}{16} \\\\  & ={sec}^{2}⁡t-{tan}^{2}⁡t \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, for any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{f}(t,y) & =⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{\\color{blue}\\operatorname{sec}t},y,\\hspace{0.167em}4\\operatorname{tan}t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2})$ and $y\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nTherefore, the first component of $\\mathbf{f}$ is ${\\color{blue}\\operatorname{sec}t}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365426 :question/problem "$$\n\\mathbf{f}(t,z)=⟨\\text{_____},\\hspace{0.222em}\\operatorname{sin}t,\\hspace{0.222em}z⟩\n$$\n\nThe vector-valued function $\\mathbf{f}(t,z)$ above parametrizes the elliptic cylinder defined in Cartesian coordinates as ${x}^{2}+25{y}^{2}=25.$ What is the first component of $\\mathbf{f}?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the ellipse \n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+25{y}^{2}=25\\,⟹\\,\\frac{{x}^{2}}{25}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{1}=1\n$$\n\nin $xy$-plane has the parametrization\n\n$$\n⟨x,y⟩=⟨5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩,\\,t\\in [0,2\\pi ),\n$$\n\nsince \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{25}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{1} & =\\frac{(5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}}{25}+(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2} \\\\  & ={cos}^{2}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, for any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{f}(t,z) & =⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{\\color{blue}5\\operatorname{cos}t},\\hspace{0.222em}\\operatorname{sin}t,\\hspace{0.222em}z⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in [0,2\\pi )$ and $z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nTherefore, the first component of $\\mathbf{f}$ is ${\\color{blue}5\\operatorname{cos}t}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365433 :question/problem "$$\n\\mathbf{f}(t,y)=⟨\\text{_____},y,\\hspace{0.222em}\\hspace{0.222em}2\\operatorname{sin}t⟩\n$$\n\nThe vector-valued function $\\mathbf{f}(t,y)$ above parametrizes the elliptic cylinder defined in Cartesian coordinates as ${x}^{2}+4{z}^{2}=16.$ What is the first component of $\\mathbf{f}?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the ellipse \n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+4{z}^{2}=16\\,⟹\\,\\frac{{x}^{2}}{16}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{4}=1\n$$\n\nin $xz$-plane has the parametrization\n\n$$\n⟨x,z⟩=⟨4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩,\\,t\\in [0,2\\pi ),\n$$\n\nsince \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{16}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{4} & =\\frac{(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}}{16}+\\frac{(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}}{4} \\\\  & ={cos}^{2}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, for any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{f}(t,y) & =⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{\\color{blue}4\\operatorname{cos}t},\\hspace{0.222em}y,\\hspace{0.222em}2\\operatorname{sin}t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in [0,2\\pi )$ and $y\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nTherefore, the first component of $\\mathbf{f}$ is ${\\color{blue}4\\operatorname{cos}t}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365440 :question/problem "$$\n\\mathbf{f}(t,y)=⟨\\text{_____},y,\\hspace{0.222em}\\hspace{0.222em}2\\operatorname{sin}t⟩\n$$\n\nThe vector-valued function $\\mathbf{f}(t,y)$ above parametrizes the elliptic cylinder defined in Cartesian coordinates as $4{x}^{2}+25{z}^{2}=100.$ What is the first component of $\\mathbf{f}?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the ellipse \n\n$$\n4{x}^{2}+25{z}^{2}=100\\,⟹\\,\\frac{{x}^{2}}{25}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{4}=1\n$$\n\nin $xz$-plane has the parametrization\n\n$$\n⟨x,z⟩=⟨5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩,\\,t\\in [0,2\\pi ),\n$$\n\nsince \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{25}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{4} & =\\frac{(5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}}{25}+\\frac{(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}}{4} \\\\  & ={cos}^{2}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, for any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{f}(t,y) & =⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{\\color{blue}5\\operatorname{cos}t},\\hspace{0.222em}y,\\hspace{0.222em}2\\operatorname{sin}t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in [0,2\\pi )$ and $y\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nTherefore, the first component of $\\mathbf{f}$ is ${\\color{blue}5\\operatorname{cos}t}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365547 :question/problem "Given that $B=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 1 & k+2 & -5 \\\\ 4 & -1 & 6\\end{bmatrix}$ and $det(B)=70$, find the value of $k.$\n\n$k=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Let's use our formula to find an expression for the determinant in terms of $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{det}(B) & =\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 1 & k+2 & -5 \\\\ 4 & -1 & 6\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =3\\begin{vmatrix}k+2 & -5 \\\\ -1 & 6\\end{vmatrix}-0\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -5 \\\\ 4 & 6\\end{vmatrix}+2\\begin{vmatrix}1 & k+2 \\\\ 4 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =3(6\\cdot (k+2)-(-5)(-1))+2(1\\cdot (-1)-4\\cdot (k+2)) \\\\  & =3(6k+12-5)+2(-1-4k-8) \\\\  & =3(6k+7)+2(-4k-9) \\\\  & =18k+21-8k-18 \\\\  & =10k+3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the determinant of the matrix is $70,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}10k+3 & =70 \\\\ 10k & =67 \\\\ k & =\\frac{67}{10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365550 :question/problem "Find, in **ascending order**, the values ​​of $x$ for which the following matrix is singular.\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}x+2 & 2x & 4 \\\\ 3 & x & x+1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nIn ascending order, $x=$\n{{field-1}} and $x=$\n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "First, let's use our formula to find an expression for the determinant in terms of $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}x+2 & 2x & 4 \\\\ 3 & x & x+1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 3\\end{vmatrix} & =(x+2)\\begin{vmatrix}x & x+1 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{vmatrix}-2x\\begin{vmatrix}3 & x+1 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{vmatrix}+4\\begin{vmatrix}3 & x \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(x+2)(3x)-2x(8-x)+4(-x) \\\\  & =3x(x+2)-2x(8-x)-4x \\\\  & =3{x}^{2}+6x-16x+2{x}^{2}-4x \\\\  & =5{x}^{2}-14x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the matrix is singular, the determinant is $0,$ and we have\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5{x}^{2}-14x & =0 \\\\ x(5x-14) & =0 \\\\ x & =0\\,\\text{or}\\,x=\\frac{14}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, in ascending order, the solutions are $x=0$ and $x=\\frac{14}{5}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365555 :question/problem "Let $\\mathbf{u}=⟨1,-3,2⟩$ and $\\mathbf{v}=⟨4,2,-1⟩.$ Find $\\mathbf{u}\\times \\mathbf{v}.$\n\n$\\mathbf{u}\\times \\mathbf{v}=⟨${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$,$\n{{field-3}}$⟩$" :question/worked-solution "Using the determinant formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{u}\\times \\mathbf{v} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ 1 & -3 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 2 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-3 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i}-\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 4 & -1\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{j}+\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -3 \\\\ 4 & 2\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =((-3)(-1)-(2)(2))\\mathbf{i}-((1)(-1)-(2)(4))\\mathbf{j}+((1)(2)-(-3)(4))\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =(3-4)\\mathbf{i}-(-1-8)\\mathbf{j}+(2+12)\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =-1\\mathbf{i}+9\\mathbf{j}+14\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨-1,9,14⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366191 :question/problem "Let $\\mathbf{a}=⟨5,\\sqrt{2},0⟩$ and $\\mathbf{b}=⟨-1,1,0⟩.$ Find $\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b}.$" :question/worked-solution "Using the determinant formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ 5 & \\sqrt{2} & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\sqrt{2} & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i}-\\begin{vmatrix}5 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{j}+\\begin{vmatrix}5 & \\sqrt{2} \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =(\\sqrt{2}\\cdot 0-0\\cdot 1)\\mathbf{i}-(5\\cdot 0-0\\cdot (-1))\\mathbf{j}+(5\\cdot 1-\\sqrt{2}\\cdot (-1))\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =0\\cdot \\mathbf{i}-0\\cdot \\mathbf{j}+(5+\\sqrt{2})\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨0,0,5+\\sqrt{2}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366198 :question/problem "Let $\\mathbf{a}=⟨4,2,-1⟩$, $\\mathbf{b}=⟨x,2,-1⟩$ and $|\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b}\\hspace{0.167em}|=\\sqrt{20}.$ Find $x.$" :question/worked-solution "Using the determinant formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ 4 & 2 & -1 \\\\ x & 2 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i}-\\begin{vmatrix}4 & -1 \\\\ x & -1\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{j}+\\begin{vmatrix}4 & 2 \\\\ x & 2\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =(-2+2)\\mathbf{i}-(-4+x)\\mathbf{j}+(8-2x)\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨0,4-x,8-2x⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, computing the magnitude, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b}\\hspace{0.167em}| & =\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+(4-x)^{2}+(8-2x)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{16-8x+{x}^{2}+64-32x+4{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5{x}^{2}-40x+80}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, we solve for $x$ the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b}\\hspace{0.167em}| & =\\sqrt{20} \\\\ \\sqrt{5{x}^{2}-40x+80} & =\\sqrt{20} \\\\ 5{x}^{2}-40x+80 & =20 \\\\ 5{x}^{2}-40x+60 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-8x+12 & =0 \\\\ (x-2)(x-6) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are $x=2,\\,6.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366794 :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the matrix $D$ given below?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n$$\nD=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 4 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 6 & 7\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that a square matrix is singular if its determinant is equal to $0.$\n\nUsing the formula, we take the alternating sum of the entries in the top row of the matrix, multiplied by their respective minors, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(D) & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 4 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 6 & 7\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1\\begin{vmatrix}4 & 5 \\\\ 6 & 7\\end{vmatrix}-2\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 7\\end{vmatrix}+3\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 6\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1(28-30)-2(7-5)+3(6-4) \\\\  & =1\\cdot (-2)-2\\cdot 2+3\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =-2-4+6 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $det(D)=0$, the matrix is singular. \n\nTherefore, only statements “$D$ is a singular matrix” and $det(D)=0$ are true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367603 :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int 2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To solve this integral, we use the by-parts formula, given by\n\n$$\n\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x=uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe need to express $\\displaystyle \\int 2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\text{d}x$ as $\\displaystyle \\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x.$ Therefore, we set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=2x\\, & ⟹\\,{u}^{′}=2 \\\\ {v}^{′}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\, & ⟹\\,v=-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute the above into the integration by parts formula, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int 2x\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x & =2x\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)}{2}\\right)-\\int \\left(-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)}{2}\\right)\\cdot 2\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x & =-x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)+\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)}{2}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367610 :question/problem "![](images/18/18a6f3e8c2cb509bcdc576a453d0b351caf8193039a8ef9905522be8feabdfac.png)\n\nWhich of the following gives the arc length of the polar curve $r=5+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5\\theta )$ for $0\\le \\theta \\le \\pi $ (shown in blue in the diagram above)." :question/worked-solution "The arc length $L$ of a polar curve $r=r(\\theta )$ is given by\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r=5+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5\\theta ),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5\\theta ).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the arc length is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{(5+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5\\theta ))^{2}+(5\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5\\theta ))^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{25+10\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5\\theta )+{sin}^{2}⁡(5\\theta )+25{cos}^{2}⁡(5\\theta )}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{25+10\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5\\theta )+1+24{cos}^{2}⁡(5\\theta )}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{26+10\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5\\theta )+24{cos}^{2}⁡(5\\theta )}\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367659 :question/problem "The region $R$ is enclosed inside the cylinder ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1$ between the surfaces $z=0$ and $z=1.$ Express the following triple integral in cylindrical polar coordinates. \n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}{z}^{2}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}V\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,dV={∭}_{Δ}\\,f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,z)\\,r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta .\n$$\n\nIf $(r,\\theta ,z)$ are the cylindrical polar coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the formulas\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z=z.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the projection of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane:\n\n![](images/f4/f4c6f3acefa5d89fd5064de469952da3d68d379559bb4a6a4990a2ea24e4f861.png)\n\nThe region $R$ is a right vertical cylinder of radius $1$ whose axis passes through the point $(0,0)$ in the $xy$-plane, and is\n\n- bounded below by $z=0,$ and\n\n- bounded above by $z=1.$\n\nAs a result, in cylindrical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \n\n$$\n\\mathrm{Δ}=\\{(r,\\theta ,z)\\hspace{0.167em}:\\hspace{0.167em}0\\le r\\le 1,\\hspace{0.222em}0\\le \\theta <2\\pi ,\\hspace{0.222em}0\\le z\\le 1\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}{z}^{2}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{\\mathrm{Δ}}{z}^{2}\\sqrt{(r\\operatorname{cos}\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}\\theta )^{2}}\\cdot r\\hspace{0.222em}\\mathrm{d}z\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}r\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={∭}_{\\mathrm{Δ}}r{z}^{2}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}({\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta )}\\hspace{0.222em}\\mathrm{d}z\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}r\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}1}}}{\\int }_{0}^{\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}1}}}\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}{r}^{2}{z}^{2}}}\\hspace{0.222em}\\mathrm{d}z\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}r\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367666 :question/problem "The region $R$ is enclosed inside the cylinder ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=2$ between the surfaces $z=0$ and $z=\\sqrt{16-({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})}.$ Express the following triple integral in cylindrical polar coordinates. \n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1+z}}\\,\\text{d}V\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,dV={∭}_{Δ}\\,f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,z)\\,r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta .\n$$\n\nIf $(r,\\theta ,z)$ are the cylindrical polar coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the formulas\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z=z.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the projection of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane:\n\n![](images/36/36839d1fbde65e86df7335d40374fe72a5ff43ed92b89405327406a62dc8763c.png)\n\nThe region $R$ is a right vertical cylinder of radius $\\sqrt{2}$ whose axis passes through the point $(0,0)$ in the $xy$-plane, and is\n\n- bounded below by $z=0,$ and\n\n- bounded above by $z=\\sqrt{16-({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})}=\\sqrt{16-{r}^{2}}.$\n\nAs a result, in cylindrical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta ,z)\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le \\sqrt{2},\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi ,\\,0\\le z\\le \\sqrt{16-{r}^{2}}\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{z}}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{\\mathrm{Δ}}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1+z}}\\cdot r\\hspace{0.222em}\\mathrm{d}z\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}r\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}\\sqrt{2}}}}{\\int }_{0}^{\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}\\sqrt{16-{r}^{2}}}}}\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}\\frac{r}{\\sqrt{1+z}}}}\\hspace{0.222em}\\mathrm{d}z\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}r\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367673 :question/problem "The region $R$ is enclosed inside the cylinder ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=9$ for $x\\le 0,$ between the surfaces $z=x$ and $z={y}^{2}.$ Express the following triple integral in cylindrical polar coordinates. \n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}V\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,dV={∭}_{Δ}\\,f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,z)\\,r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta .\n$$\n\nIf $(r,\\theta ,z)$ are the cylindrical polar coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the formulas\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z=z.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the projection of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane:\n\n![](images/16/16b7c2f69bb20df2faafabf10368776cb69b608bb61eb8432495ae5f146e7100.png)\n\nThe region $R$ is one-half of a right vertical cylinder of radius $3$ whose axis passes through the point $(0,0)$ in the $xy$-plane, and is\n\n- bounded below by $z=x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,$ and\n\n- bounded above by $z={y}^{2}={r}^{2}{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta .$\n\nAs a result, in cylindrical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \n\n$$\n\\mathrm{Δ}=\\{(r,\\theta ,z)\\hspace{0.167em}:\\hspace{0.167em}0\\le r\\le 3,\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta <\\frac{3\\pi }{2},\\hspace{0.222em}r\\operatorname{cos}\\theta \\le z\\le {r}^{2}{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta \\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{\\mathrm{Δ}}((r\\operatorname{cos}\\theta )^{2}-(r\\operatorname{sin}\\theta )^{2})\\cdot r\\hspace{0.222em}\\mathrm{d}z\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}r\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={∭}_{\\mathrm{Δ}}r({r}^{2}{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta -{r}^{2}{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta )\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}z\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}r\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={∭}_{\\mathrm{Δ}}{r}^{3}({\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta -{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta )\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}z\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}r\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}3\\pi /2}}}{\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}r\\operatorname{cos}\\theta }}}^{{r}^{2}{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta }\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}{r}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}2\\theta }}\\hspace{0.222em}\\mathrm{d}z\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}r\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367680 :question/problem "The region $R$ is enclosed inside the cylinder ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=5$ for $y\\ge 0,$ between the surfaces $z=-1$ and $z=y+2.$ Express the following triple integral in cylindrical polar coordinates. \n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}z({x}^{2}-{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}V\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,dV={∭}_{Δ}\\,f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,z)\\,r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta .\n$$\n\nIf $(r,\\theta ,z)$ are the cylindrical polar coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the formulas\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z=z.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the projection of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane:\n\n![](images/7d/7d3ef0b0a9a2f1c9ab60b4ed09f8a0318c57f74236e404baf181e0197ef66002.png)\n\nThe region $R$ is one-half of a right vertical cylinder of radius $\\sqrt{5}$ whose axis passes through the point $(0,0)$ in the $xy$-plane, and is\n\n- bounded below by $z=-1,$ and\n\n- bounded above by $z=y+2=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +2.$\n\nAs a result, in cylindrical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \n\n$$\n\\mathrm{Δ}=\\{(r,\\theta ,z)\\hspace{0.167em}:\\hspace{0.167em}0\\le r\\le \\sqrt{5},\\hspace{0.222em}0\\le \\theta \\le \\pi ,\\hspace{0.222em}-1\\le z\\le r\\operatorname{sin}\\theta +2\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}z({x}^{2}-{y}^{2})\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{\\mathrm{Δ}}z((r\\operatorname{cos}\\theta )^{2}-(r\\operatorname{sin}\\theta )^{2})r\\hspace{0.222em}\\mathrm{d}z\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}r\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={∭}_{\\mathrm{Δ}}rz({r}^{2}{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta -{r}^{2}{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta )r\\hspace{0.222em}\\mathrm{d}z\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}r\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={∭}_{\\mathrm{Δ}}{r}^{3}z({\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta -{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta )\\mathrm{d}z\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}r\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}\\pi }}}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{5}}{\\int }_{-1}^{\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}r\\operatorname{sin}\\theta +2}}}\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}{r}^{3}z\\operatorname{cos}2\\theta }}\\hspace{0.222em}\\mathrm{d}z\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}r\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367687 :question/problem "Evaluate the triple integral \n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}x\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}V\n$$\n\nover the region $R$ enclosed inside the cylinder ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=3$ for $x\\ge 0$ and between the surfaces $z=y$ and $z=y+4.$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,dV={∭}_{Δ}\\,f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,z)\\,r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta .\n$$\n\nIf $(r,\\theta ,z)$ are the cylindrical polar coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the formulas\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z=z.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the projection of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane:\n\n![](images/e2/e289cfbd210112a619018a7b905f7f2b1a9859ecb84b2517dd745705624d79d7.png)\n\nThe region $R$ is a right vertical cylinder of radius $\\sqrt{3}$ whose axis passes through the point $(0,0)$ in the $xy$-plane, and is\n\n- bounded below by $z=y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ and\n\n- bounded above by $z=y+4=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +4.$\n\nAs a result, in cylindrical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta ,z)\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le \\sqrt{3},\\,-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta <\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\le z\\le r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +4\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}x\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{Δ}r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\sqrt{(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}}\\,r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{3}}{\\int }_{r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }^{r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +4}{r}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{3}}{r}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta [z]_{z=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }^{z=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +4}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{3}}{r}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta (r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +4-r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{3}}4{r}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}[{r}^{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ]_{r=0}^{r=\\sqrt{3}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}(\\sqrt{3})^{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}9\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =[9\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ]_{\\theta =-\\pi /2}^{\\theta =\\pi /2} \\\\  & =9(1-(-1)) \\\\  & =18.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367694 :question/problem "Evaluate the triple integral \n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}\\frac{z}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}V\n$$\n\nover the region $R$ enclosed inside the cylinder ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=9$ and between the surfaces $z=0$ and $z=\\sqrt{9-({x}^{2}-{y}^{2})}.$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,dV={∭}_{Δ}\\,f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,z)\\,r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta .\n$$\n\nIf $(r,\\theta ,z)$ are the cylindrical polar coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the formulas\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z=z.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the projection of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane:\n\n![](images/9c/9c4ea0ba92e19a201e3944defa6deb0bc1ee76971adaa809521722b6ac31241f.png)\n\nThe region $R$ is a right vertical cylinder of radius $3$ whose axis passes through the point $(0,0)$ in the $xy$-plane, and is\n\n- bounded below by $z=0,$ and\n\n- bounded above by $$\nz=\\sqrt{9-({x}^{2}-{y}^{2})}=\\sqrt{9-{r}^{2}({\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta -{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta )}=\\sqrt{9-{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}(2\\theta )}.\n$$\n\nAs a result, in cylindrical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta ,z)\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 3,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi ,\\,0\\le z\\le \\sqrt{9-{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )}\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}\\frac{z}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{\\mathrm{Δ}}\\frac{z}{\\sqrt{{r}^{2}{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}\\theta }}\\hspace{0.222em}r\\hspace{0.222em}\\mathrm{d}z\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}r\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{9-{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}(2\\theta )}}z\\hspace{0.222em}\\mathrm{d}z\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}r\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{3}{[\\frac{1}{2}{z}^{2}]}_{z=0}^{z=\\sqrt{9-{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}(2\\theta )}}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}r\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{3}\\frac{1}{2}{(\\sqrt{9-{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}(2\\theta )})}^{2}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}r\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{3}\\frac{1}{2}(9-{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}(2\\theta ))\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}r\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{[\\frac{9}{2}r-\\frac{1}{6}{r}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}(2\\theta )]}_{r=0}^{r=3}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\frac{9}{2}(3)-\\frac{1}{6}(3)^{3}\\operatorname{cos}(2\\theta )\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\frac{27}{2}-\\frac{9}{2}\\operatorname{cos}(2\\theta )\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={[\\frac{27}{2}\\theta -\\frac{9}{4}\\operatorname{sin}(2\\theta )]}_{\\theta =0}^{\\theta =2\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{27}{2}(2\\pi -0)-\\frac{9}{4}(0-0) \\\\  & =27\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367701 :question/problem "Evaluate the triple integral \n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}V\n$$\n\nover the region $R$ enclosed inside the cylinder ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1$ and between the surfaces $z=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}$ and $z=1.$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,dV={∭}_{Δ}\\,f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,z)\\,r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta .\n$$\n\nIf $(r,\\theta ,z)$ are the cylindrical polar coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the formulas\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z=z.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the projection of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane:\n\n![](images/f4/f4c6f3acefa5d89fd5064de469952da3d68d379559bb4a6a4990a2ea24e4f861.png)\n\nThe region $R$ is a right vertical cylinder of radius $1$ whose axis passes through the point $(0,0)$ in the $xy$-plane, and is\n\n- bounded below by  $$\nz=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}=\\sqrt{{r}^{2}{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +{r}^{2}{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta }=r,\n$$  and\n\n- bounded above by $z=1.$\n\nAs a result, in cylindrical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta ,z)\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 1,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi ,\\,r\\le z\\le 1\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{Δ}({(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}^{2}+{(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}^{2})\\,r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{r}^{1}{r}^{3}\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{1}{r}^{3}[z]_{z=r}^{z=1}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{1}{r}^{3}(1-r)\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{1}{r}^{3}-{r}^{4}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{[\\frac{1}{4}{r}^{4}-\\frac{1}{5}{r}^{5}]}_{r=0}^{r=1}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\frac{1}{4}-\\frac{1}{5}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\frac{1}{20}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={[\\frac{1}{20}\\theta ]}_{\\theta =0}^{\\theta =2\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{20}(2\\pi -0) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367708 :question/problem "Evaluate the triple integral \n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}2xz\\,\\text{d}V\n$$\n\nover the region $R$ enclosed inside the cylinder ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1$ for $y\\ge 0$ and between the surfaces $z=-x$ and $z=x+2.$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,dV={∭}_{Δ}\\,f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,z)\\,r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta .\n$$\n\nIf $(r,\\theta ,z)$ are the cylindrical polar coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the formulas\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z=z.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the projection of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane:\n\n![](images/f8/f837d76caa5a6fd2c2de9a628a77c211fc12be6733fec4f1d3332b9c8ff0991c.png)\n\nThe region $R$ is a right vertical cylinder of radius $1$ whose axis passes through the point $(0,0)$ in the $xy$-plane, and is\n\n- bounded below by $z=-x=-r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,$ and\n\n- bounded above by $z=x+2=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +2.$\n\nAs a result, in cylindrical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \n\n$$\n\\mathrm{Δ}=\\{(r,\\theta ,z)\\hspace{0.167em}:\\hspace{0.167em}0\\le r\\le 1,\\hspace{0.222em}0\\le \\theta <\\pi ,\\hspace{0.222em}-r\\operatorname{cos}\\theta \\le z\\le r\\operatorname{cos}\\theta +2\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}2xz\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{\\mathrm{Δ}}2z\\cdot r\\operatorname{cos}\\theta \\cdot r\\hspace{0.222em}\\mathrm{d}z\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}r\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{-r\\operatorname{cos}\\theta }^{r\\operatorname{cos}\\theta +2}2z{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}\\theta \\hspace{0.222em}\\mathrm{d}z\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}r\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{1}{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}\\theta [{z}^{2}]_{z=-r\\operatorname{cos}\\theta }^{z=r\\operatorname{cos}\\theta +2}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}r\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{1}{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}\\theta ({(r\\operatorname{cos}\\theta +2)}^{2}-{(-r\\operatorname{cos}\\theta )}^{2})\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}r\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{1}{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}\\theta ({r}^{2}{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +4r\\operatorname{cos}\\theta +4-{r}^{2}{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta )\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}r\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{1}4{r}^{3}{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +4{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}\\theta \\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}r\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }[{r}^{4}{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +\\frac{4}{3}{r}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}\\theta ]_{r=0}^{r=1}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +\\frac{4}{3}\\operatorname{cos}\\theta \\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\frac{1}{2}(1+\\operatorname{cos}(2\\theta ))+\\frac{4}{3}\\operatorname{cos}\\theta \\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}(2\\theta )+\\frac{4}{3}\\operatorname{cos}\\theta \\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={[\\frac{1}{2}\\theta +\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sin}(2\\theta )+\\frac{4}{3}\\operatorname{sin}\\theta ]}_{\\theta =0}^{\\theta =\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\pi -0)+\\frac{1}{4}(0-0)+\\frac{4}{3}(0-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367715 :question/problem "Evaluate the triple integral \n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}4\\,\\text{d}V\n$$\n\nover the region $R$ enclosed inside the cylinder ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=2$ for $y\\ge 0$ and between the surfaces $z=0$ and $z=6-2x.$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,dV={∭}_{Δ}\\,f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,z)\\,r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta .\n$$\n\nIf $(r,\\theta ,z)$ are the cylindrical polar coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the formulas\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z=z.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the projection of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane:\n\n![](images/6f/6fe710e758ccda9a826d79f8219dc82b25f8cc39f65d92f02a6c28594c428a96.png)\n\nThe region $R$ is a right vertical cylinder of radius $\\sqrt{2}$ whose axis passes through the point $(0,0)$ in the $xy$-plane, and is\n\n- bounded below by $z=0,$ and\n\n- bounded above by $z=6-2x=6-2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .$\n\nAs a result, in cylindrical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \n\n$$\n\\mathrm{Δ}=\\{(r,\\theta ,z)\\hspace{0.167em}:\\hspace{0.167em}0\\le r\\le \\sqrt{2},\\hspace{0.222em}0\\le \\theta <\\pi ,\\hspace{0.222em}0\\le z\\le 6-2r\\operatorname{cos}\\theta \\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}4\\,\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{Δ}4\\,r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}{\\int }_{0}^{6-2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }4r\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}4r[z]_{z=0}^{z=6-2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}4r(6-2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}24r-8{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{[12{r}^{2}-\\frac{8}{3}{r}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ]}_{r=0}^{r=\\sqrt{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }12(\\sqrt{2})^{2}-\\frac{8}{3}(\\sqrt{2})^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }24-\\frac{16\\sqrt{2}}{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={[24\\theta -\\frac{16\\sqrt{2}}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ]}_{\\theta =0}^{\\theta =\\pi } \\\\  & =24(\\pi -0)-\\frac{16\\sqrt{2}}{3}(0-0) \\\\  & =24\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367722 :question/problem "If the solid region $R$ is given by \n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(x+3)^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 9,\\,-8\\le z\\le x+2\\},\n$$\n\nthen $$\n\\displaystyle {∭}_{R}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1}}\\,\\text{d}V=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,dV={∭}_{Δ}\\,f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,z)\\,r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta .\n$$\n\nIf $(r,\\theta ,z)$ are the cylindrical polar coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the formulas\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z=z.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the projection of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane:\n\n![](images/3c/3c03dde21942c60de527ace896bb51ad8070781126d72f5be5e2f6069700736d.png)\n\nThe region $R$ is a right vertical cylinder of radius $3$ whose axis passes through the point $(-3,0)$ in the $xy$-plane, and is\n\n- bounded below by $z=-8,$ and\n\n- bounded above by $z=x+2=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +2.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's write the equation $(x+3)^{2}+{y}^{2}=9$ in polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+3)^{2}+{y}^{2} & =9 \\\\ {(r\\operatorname{cos}\\theta +3)}^{2}+{(r\\operatorname{sin}\\theta )}^{2} & =9 \\\\ {r}^{2}{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +6r\\operatorname{cos}\\theta +9+{r}^{2}{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta  & =9 \\\\ {r}^{2}({\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta )+6r\\operatorname{cos}\\theta  & =0 \\\\ {r}^{2}+6r\\operatorname{cos}\\theta  & =0 \\\\ r(r+6\\operatorname{cos}\\theta ) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire circle, we must have $r=-6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .$\n\nAs a result, in cylindrical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta ,z)\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le -6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{3\\pi }{2},\\,-8\\le z\\le r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +2\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1}}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{\\mathrm{Δ}}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{(r\\operatorname{cos}\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}\\theta )^{2}+1}}\\cdot r\\hspace{0.222em}\\mathrm{d}z\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}r\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={∭}_{\\mathrm{Δ}}\\frac{r}{\\sqrt{{r}^{2}({\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta )+1}}\\hspace{0.222em}\\mathrm{d}z\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}r\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}-6\\operatorname{cos}\\theta }}}{\\int }_{-8}^{\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}r\\operatorname{cos}\\theta +2}}}\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}\\frac{r}{\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+1}}}}\\hspace{0.222em}\\mathrm{d}z\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}r\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367729 :question/problem "If the solid region $R$ is given by \n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\hspace{0.167em}:\\hspace{0.167em}(x-3)^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 9,\\hspace{0.222em}y\\le 0,\\hspace{0.222em}y\\le z\\le 6\\},\n$$\n\nthen $$\n\\displaystyle {∭}_{R}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{z+1}}\\,\\text{d}V=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,dV={∭}_{Δ}\\,f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,z)\\,r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta .\n$$\n\nIf $(r,\\theta ,z)$ are the cylindrical polar coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the formulas\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z=z.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the projection of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane:\n\n![](images/cc/ccc5cab37aed89c0d701ce0195414796211ddc73a498eaaa1ff145a45c42e9fd.png)\n\nThe region $R$ is one-half of a right vertical cylinder of radius $3$ whose axis passes through the point $(3,0)$ in the $xy$-plane, and is\n\n- bounded below by $z=y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ and\n\n- bounded above by $z=6.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write the $(x-3)^{2}+{y}^{2}=9$ in polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-3)^{2}+{y}^{2} & =9 \\\\ (r\\operatorname{cos}\\theta -3)^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}\\theta )^{2} & =3 \\\\ {r}^{2}{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta -6r\\operatorname{cos}\\theta +9+{r}^{2}{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta  & =9 \\\\ {r}^{2}({\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta )-6r\\operatorname{cos}\\theta  & =0 \\\\ {r}^{2}-6r\\operatorname{cos}\\theta  & =0 \\\\ r(r-6\\operatorname{cos}\\theta ) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire semicircle, we must have $r=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .$\n\nAs a result, in cylindrical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \n\n$$\n\\mathrm{Δ}=\\{(r,\\theta ,z)\\hspace{0.167em}:\\hspace{0.167em}0\\le r\\le 6\\operatorname{cos}\\theta ,\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi ,\\hspace{0.222em}r\\operatorname{sin}\\theta \\le z\\le 6\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{z+1}}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{\\mathrm{Δ}}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{z+1}}\\cdot r\\hspace{0.222em}\\mathrm{d}z\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}r\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{3\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}6\\operatorname{cos}\\theta }}}{\\int }_{\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}r\\operatorname{sin}\\theta }}}^{6}\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}\\frac{r}{\\sqrt{z+1}}}}\\hspace{0.222em}\\mathrm{d}z\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}r\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367921 :question/problem "Given that $f(x,y,z)={y}^{2}{z}^{2}+xz,$ find $\\frac{∂f}{∂z},$ where $(x,y,z)$ are Cartesian coordinates and $(r,\\theta ,z)$ are the corresponding cylindrical polar coordinates." :question/worked-solution "Applying the multivariable chain rule for the function $f(x,y,z)$ with three independent variables $r,\\theta ,$ and $z,$ where $x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ and $z=z,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂z} & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂z}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂z}+\\frac{∂f}{∂z}\\cdot \\frac{∂z}{∂z} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂f}{∂z}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $z:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂z}=2{y}^{2}z+x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $r,\\theta ,$ and $z,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂z} & =2{r}^{2}z{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186368376 :question/problem "A body of mass $64\\,\\text{kg}$ is moving in a straight line along the $x$-axis under the action of a single force $F,$ measured in Newtons $(\\text{N}).$  At time $t>0$ seconds, the position of the body is given by $s(t)=\\sqrt{t}$ meters.\n\nCalculate the magnitude of the force at time $t=4$ seconds. Do not round the answer.\n\nThe magnitude of the force equals\n{{field-1}} $\\text{N}.$" :question/worked-solution "According to Newton’s second law,\n\n$$\nF(t)=ma(t),\n$$\n\n \nwhere $F(t)$ is the force, $m$ is the mass, and $a(t)$ is the acceleration.\n\nTo find the velocity $v(t),$ we differentiate the displacement $s(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\sqrt{t}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{1/2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{-1/2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the acceleration $a(t),$ we differentiate the velocity $v(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{-1/2}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}{t}^{-3/2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4{(\\sqrt{t})}^{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, the acceleration at $t=4\\,\\text{s}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(4) & =-\\frac{1}{4{(\\sqrt{4})}^{3}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4({2}^{3})} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4\\cdot 8} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{32}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the force in Newtons is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(4) & =ma(4) \\\\  & =64\\cdot -\\frac{1}{32} \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the magnitude of the force is\n\n$$\n|F(4)|=|-2|=2\\,\\text{N}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186368379 :question/problem "A body of mass $2\\,\\text{kg}$ is moving in a straight line along the $x$-axis under the action of a single force $F,$ measured in Newtons.\nAt time $t\\ge 0$ seconds, the velocity of the body is given by $v(t)=4{t}^{2}-32t+64$ meters per second.\n\nCalculate the time ${t}_{∗}$ at which the force acting on the body has zero magnitude. Do not round the answer.\n\n${t}_{∗}=$\n\n{{field-1}}  $\\text{s}$" :question/worked-solution "According to Newton’s second law\n\n$$\nF(t)=ma(t),\n$$\n\n \nwhere $F(t)$ is the force, $m$ is the mass, and $a(t)$ is the acceleration. \n\nTo find the acceleration $a(t),$ we differentiate the velocity $v(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4{t}^{2}-32t+64) \\\\  & =8t-32\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the mass is always positive, the force has zero magnitude when the acceleration equals zero. So, we set $a(t)=0$ and solve for $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8t-32 & =0 \\\\ 8t & =32 \\\\ t & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the force has zero magnitude at ${t}_{∗}=4\\,\\text{s}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186368382 :question/problem "A body of mass $4\\,\\text{kg}$ is moving in a straight line along the $x$-axis under the action of a single force $F,$ measured in Newtons. At time $t\\ge 0$ seconds, the position of the body is given by $$\n\\displaystyle s(t)=\\frac{1}{3}{t}^{4}-\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2}\n$$ meters.\n\nCalculate the time ${t}_{∗}$ at which the force acting on the body has zero magnitude. Do not round the answer.\n\n${t}_{∗}=$\n\n{{field-1}} $\\text{s}$" :question/worked-solution "According to Newton’s second law\n\n$$\nF=ma,\n$$\n\n \nwhere $F(t)$ is the force, $m$ is the mass, and $a(t)$ is the acceleration. \n\nTo find the velocity $v(t),$ we differentiate the displacement $s(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\,(\\frac{1}{3}{t}^{4}-\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}{t}^{3}-t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the acceleration $a(t),$ we differentiate the velocity $v(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\,(\\frac{4}{3}{t}^{3}-t) \\\\  & =4{t}^{2}-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the mass is always positive, the force has zero magnitude when the acceleration equals zero. So, we set $a(t)=0$ and solve for $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{t}^{2}-1 & =0 \\\\ 4{t}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {t}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ t & =\\pm \\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $t\\ge 0,$ we disregard the negative solution. \n\nTherefore, the force has zero magnitude at ${t}_{∗}=\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{s}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186368385 :question/problem "A body of mass $2\\,\\text{kg}$ is moving in a straight line along the $x$-axis under the action of a single force $F,$ measured in Newtons. At time $t\\ge 0$ seconds, the position of the body is given by $s(t)=\\frac{1}{8}{e}^{-2t}(2{t}^{2}+8t-9)$ meters.\n\nCalculate the time ${t}_{∗}$ at which the force acting on the body has zero magnitude. Do not round the answer.\n\n${t}_{∗}=$\n\n{{field-1}} $\\text{s}$" :question/worked-solution "According to Newton’s second law\n\n$$\nF=ma,\n$$\n\n \nwhere $F(t)$ is the force, $m$ is the mass, and $a(t)$ is the acceleration. \n\nTo find the velocity $v(t),$ we differentiate the displacement $s(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\,[\\frac{1}{8}{e}^{-2t}(2{t}^{2}+8t-9)] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}[{e}^{-2t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{t}^{2}+8t-9)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{-2t})\\cdot (2{t}^{2}+8t-9)] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}[{e}^{-2t}(4t+8)-2{e}^{-2t}(2{t}^{2}+8t-9)] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}{e}^{-2t}[4t+8-2(2{t}^{2}+8t-9)] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}{e}^{-2t}(4t+8-4{t}^{2}-16t+18) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}{e}^{-2t}(-4{t}^{2}-12t+26) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}{e}^{-2t}\\cdot 2(13-6t-2{t}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{e}^{-2t}(13-6t-2{t}^{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the acceleration $a(t),$ we differentiate the velocity $v(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\,[\\frac{1}{4}{e}^{-2t}(13-6t-2{t}^{2})] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[{e}^{-2t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(13-6t-2{t}^{2})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{-2t})\\cdot (13-6t-2{t}^{2})] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[{e}^{-2t}(-6-4t)-2{e}^{-2t}(13-6t-2{t}^{2})] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{e}^{-2t}(-6-4t-26+12t+4{t}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{e}^{-2t}(4{t}^{2}+8t-32) \\\\  & ={e}^{-2t}({t}^{2}+2t-8)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the mass is always positive, the force has zero magnitude when the acceleration equals zero. So, we set $a(t)=0$ and solve for $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-2t}({t}^{2}+2t-8) & =0 \\\\ {t}^{2}+2t-8 & =0 \\\\ (t+4)(t-2) & =0 \\\\ t & =-4,2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $t\\ge 0,$ we disregard the negative solution. \n\nTherefore, the force has zero magnitude at ${t}_{∗}=2\\,\\text{s}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186368394 :question/problem "For the parametric curve \n\n$$\nx=\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{t}},\\,y=\\frac{{e}^{t}}{3t},\\,-\\infty <t<\\infty ,\n$$\n\ncalculate the slope $m$ of the tangent to the curve at the point where $t=4.$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$m=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $x$ and $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=-\\frac{1}{6}{t}^{-3/2},\\,{y}^{′}(t)=\\frac{3t{e}^{t}-3{e}^{t}}{9{t}^{2}}=\\frac{{e}^{t}(t-1)}{3{t}^{2}}.\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{{e}^{t}(t-1)}{3{t}^{2}}\\cdot (-6{t}^{3/2}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2{e}^{t}(t-1)}{\\sqrt{t}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, when $t=4,$ the slope $m$ of the tangent to the curve is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m=\\frac{dy}{dx}\\,{|}_{t\\,=\\,4} & =-\\frac{2{e}^{4}(4-1)}{\\sqrt{4}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2{e}^{4}\\cdot 3}{2} \\\\  & =-3{e}^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186368467 :question/problem "For the parametric curve \n\n$$\nx=\\sqrt{t},\\,y=3{t}^{3/2},\\,0<t<\\infty ,\n$$\n\ncalculate $\\frac{dy}{dx}$ as a function of $t.$\n\n$\\frac{dy}{dx}=$  \n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $x$ and $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{t}},\\,{y}^{′}(t)=\\frac{9}{2}\\sqrt{t}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{(\\frac{9}{2}\\sqrt{t})}{\\left(\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{t}}\\right)}=9t.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186368470 :question/problem "For the parametric curve \n\n$$\nx={e}^{2/t},\\,y=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+1}},\\,0<t<\\infty ,\n$$\n\ncalculate the slope $m$ of the tangent to the curve at the point where $t=1.$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$m=$ \n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $x$ and $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=-\\frac{2}{{t}^{2}}{e}^{2/t},\\,{y}^{′}(t)=-\\frac{t}{({t}^{2}+1)^{3/2}}.\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{\\left(-\\frac{t}{({t}^{2}+1)^{3/2}}\\right)}{(-\\frac{2}{{t}^{2}}{e}^{2/t})} \\\\  & =\\frac{{t}^{3}}{2({t}^{2}+1)^{3/2}\\,{e}^{2/t}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, when $t=1,$ the slope $m$ of the tangent to the curve is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m=\\frac{dy}{dx}\\,{|}_{t\\,=\\,1} & =\\frac{{1}^{3}}{2({1}^{2}+1)^{3/2}\\,{e}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2(2)^{3/2}\\,{e}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4\\sqrt{2}\\,{e}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{8{e}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186368473 :question/problem "![](images/d8/d8a2a7761f3bacee21d61c5212864cf5f4fe2e44198789b80f1655af73d78d53.png)\n\nFind the arc length $L$ of the polar curve $r=9\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ for $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{4}$ (shown in blue in the diagram above). Do not round the answer.\n\n$L=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The arc length $L$ of a polar curve $r=r(\\theta )$ is given by\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r=9\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }=-9\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the arc length is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\sqrt{{(9\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}^{2}+{(-9\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\sqrt{81{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +81{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\sqrt{81}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =9{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}1\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =9\\theta \\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi /4} \\\\  & =9(\\frac{\\pi }{4}-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{9\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186368628 :question/problem "![](images/94/9434dda63c08fc1202d16ab5410fe7061050515876ffa473d8720db39079af3e.png)\n\nFor the parametric curve \n\n$$\nx={t}^{3}+{t}^{2},\\,y={t}^{2},\\,-\\infty <t<\\infty ,\n$$\n\nshown above, calculate the slope of the tangent to the curve at the point where $t=2.$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $x$ and $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=3{t}^{2}+2t,\\,{y}^{′}(t)=2t.\n$$\n\n \n\nSo we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{2t}{3{t}^{2}+2t} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3t+2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, when $t=2,$ the slope of the tangent to the curve is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{2}{3(2)+2} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{6+2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
